{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 18, :comments ["<b>And Joseph said, etc.: Not so, my father.</b> The reason he drew out all these words — ״Not so, my father, for this one is the firstborn״ — and then returned again to say ״Put your right hand״ etc.:" "Now Joseph was uncertain about one of two possibilities: the first is that Jacob thought the boys were taken by Joseph's hand at the time of blessing, and the matter is well known — that he would take the firstborn in his right hand and the younger in his left, and accordingly the firstborn would be to Jacob's left and the younger to his right. Therefore he crossed his hands, for Menasseh is the firstborn, and his father would place him at his right hand. And the second way is that with the intended purpose, he crossed his hands in order to place his right hand on the head of the younger. Therefore he said to him at the outset: if his intention is to suppose that their father took them with the firstborn at Joseph's right hand, he said <b>Not so, my father</b> — as you think, that this one is the firstborn. And if you know that the firstborn is arranged at your right, etc. — but you are withholding your right hand from the firstborn, therefore he said <b>Put your right hand upon his head</b>, meaning, why do you withhold the good from its rightful owner, when you already know what caused this matter."], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 48, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:50:08.935463560Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}